          Milwaukee is built for the trades and for maximum torque; Makita is built for comfort, refinement and the broadest range on one battery. Choose by the tools you will actually buy, not by which brand wins a single test.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee and Makita are both excellent, and the wrong question is which one is better overall. The right question is which platform fits the work you do. Milwaukee is built around the trades: plumbers, electricians, mechanics and general contractors, with a deep line of specialty tools on its M18 and M12 batteries and a reputation for high-torque FUEL tools. Makita is built around refinement and breadth: light, balanced, low-vibration tools on its 18V LXT battery, a very large catalogue that includes outdoor power equipment, and a separate 40V XGT platform for heavier work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a tradesperson whose specialty tools exist in Milwaukee's line and nowhere else, choose Milwaukee. If you use tools all day and care about weight, balance, noise and vibration, or you want one battery for a wide range of tools including a mower, blower and chainsaw, choose Makita. If you are a homeowner choosing a first drill kit, either will serve you for years, and price and availability at your local store are reasonable tie-breakers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty tilts slightly toward Milwaukee on tools. Milwaukee's standard tool warranty is 5 years, while Makita's US limited warranty is 3 years on tools, batteries and chargers. On batteries the two are closer, since most M18 REDLITHIUM packs carry 3 years. Check the specific product, because both brands have exceptions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, the decision is really about a battery platform rather than a single tool, because the batteries are what you will keep buying into. Our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">best cordless tool brand</a> covers the wider field, and our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> and <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a> comparisons fill in the third major option.
          </p>

