        <div id="companies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Two Different Philosophies
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee Tool has been making power tools since 1924 and is best known historically for inventing the Sawzall reciprocating saw. Today it is owned by Techtronic Industries, the same group behind Ryobi and RIDGID, and its cordless strategy is aimed squarely at professional trades. That shows in the catalogue. Alongside drills, drivers and saws, Milwaukee sells press tools, crimpers, cable cutters, drain cleaners, pipe threaders and inspection cameras, many of them on batteries, and it designs tools around the jobs a specific trade does every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita began in Japan in 1915 as an electric motor repair company and has built power tools for more than a century. Its reputation rests on build quality and feel. In our <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a> comparison we describe Makita tools as feeling engineered rather than assembled, and that is the usual verdict: lighter, better balanced, quieter and lower in vibration than many competitors on comparable tools. Makita's catalogue is also unusually broad, especially outside the core drill and saw categories.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those philosophies lead to different strengths. Milwaukee tends to push hardest on peak performance and on niche trade tools. Makita tends to push on comfort over a full working day and on covering as many categories as possible with one battery. Neither approach is better in the abstract; they suit different users.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands make professional-grade tools, both have consumer-friendly entry kits, and both have service networks for out-of-warranty repairs, though availability varies by region. The practical difference most buyers notice first is not quality but which tools exist on each platform and how they feel in the hand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Battery Platforms: M18 and M12 vs LXT, XGT and CXT
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee's lineup is simple. M18 is the main 18V platform and, on Milwaukee's own product pages, features more than 200 solutions, from drills to lights to outdoor equipment. M12 is the compact 12V platform, and it is stronger than most compact lines, with a large range of small trade tools such as ratchets, PEX expanders, inspection cameras and right-angle drills. The two use different batteries but can share some chargers. FUEL is Milwaukee's label for its top brushless tools rather than a separate battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita's lineup has more layers. 18V LXT is the core platform and one of the largest cordless systems available, with hundreds of tools; Makita said it had more than 250 LXT products as far back as 2020. Makita also makes 36V tools that run on two LXT packs in series, which extends LXT into higher-power tools such as mowers and large saws without a new battery. 40V max XGT is a separate, higher-power platform launched in the US in 2021, and 12V max CXT is Makita's compact line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important detail is that XGT and LXT batteries are not interchangeable. Makita sells an adaptor that lets an XGT charger charge LXT packs, but LXT batteries do not power XGT tools. That means choosing Makita is really two decisions: LXT for the widest choice of everyday tools, and XGT only when you need its extra power, as we explain in the <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee has no direct XGT equivalent. Its approach is to push more power out of M18 with FUEL tools and high-output batteries, and to put the heaviest jobs on its separate MX FUEL equipment line, which covers items such as cut-off saws, breakers and core drills rather than everyday hand tools. For most users, M18 with high-output packs covers the same ground XGT does on Makita.
          </p>

          <p>
            As with any brand, the batteries are proprietary and physically incompatible across brands. Third-party adapters exist, but they bypass the communication between pack and tool and void warranties. Our guide to <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/">cordless tool batteries</a> explains amp-hours, high-output cells and why packs differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tool-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tool Range and Trade Specialties
          </h2>

          <p>
            For core tools, both brands are complete. Drills, impact drivers, hammer drills, circular saws, reciprocating saws, grinders, multi-tools, jigsaws, routers, lights and vacuums all exist on M18 and on LXT, usually in several versions at different price and power levels. A homeowner or general contractor will not run out of options on either platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference shows at the edges. Milwaukee is the stronger choice for plumbers and electricians, where tools such as press tools, PEX expansion tools, cable cutters, crimpers and battery drain cleaners are central to the job and where M12 offers compact versions that fit tight spaces. Mechanics also tend to favour Milwaukee's M12 and M18 ratchets and impact wrenches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita is the stronger choice for woodworkers, finish carpenters and anyone who values a wide spread of less common tools, from planers and trim routers to specialty saws and garden equipment. Makita's LXT range is often described as having a cordless version of almost every tool, which matters if you want one battery to cover as much as possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before choosing, list the five or six tools you will actually buy in the next few years and check that each exists on the platform at a price you are happy with. That list matters more than any total tool count, because a platform with 300 tools is no use if the one you need is missing or only available by special order.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check your must-have tools before you check the brand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a specialty tool you rely on only exists on one platform, that decides it. Count the tools you will buy, not the tools the brand makes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="feel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Power, Weight and Feel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee's M18 FUEL tools have a reputation for top-end power. In our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> comparison we note that the M18 FUEL drill consistently ranks among the most powerful in independent torque tests, and that the M18 FUEL impact driver edges out the competition in most performance tests. If maximum torque in heavy drilling and fastening is your priority, Milwaukee is a strong choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita's advantage is how the tools feel over a full day. Makita tools are widely described as lighter, better balanced, quieter and lower in vibration than comparable tools from most rivals, and that is consistent with our DeWalt vs Makita assessment. For overhead work, repetitive fastening and long finish-carpentry days, those differences reduce fatigue in a way a spec sheet does not capture.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners and many pros, the power gap at the top end does not matter in practice. A current brushless drill from either brand will drive a long lag screw, bore large holes and mix thinset. The differences show up at the limits, and only you know how often you work there.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can, hold both brands' drills and impact drivers in a store with a battery attached. Grip shape, trigger feel and balance are personal, and a tool you like holding is a tool you will use well. We have not run our own torque tests for this comparison, so treat performance claims here as the brands' reputations and independent reviewers' findings rather than our measurements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ope" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Outdoor Power Equipment
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one battery for your workshop and your yard, this is where Makita has a real edge. Makita has offered outdoor power equipment on its LXT battery for many years, including string trimmers, blowers, hedge trimmers and chainsaws, and its 36V tools that run on two LXT packs extend that to mowers and larger saws. XGT adds more powerful outdoor tools on top. The result is a very broad yard lineup that runs on the same batteries as your drill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee also sells M18 FUEL outdoor equipment, including string trimmers, blowers, hedge trimmers, chainsaws and mowers, and its QUIK-LOK attachment system lets one power head drive several attachments. Milwaukee's outdoor range is narrower than Makita's but strong in the categories it covers, and it shares batteries with the rest of the M18 line.
          </p>

          <p>
            If outdoor equipment is a big part of your plan, compare the specific machines you want. For a large yard, check mower deck sizes, runtime and how many batteries each tool needs, because big outdoor tools often draw two packs at once. Our <a href="/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/">battery-powered lawn mower</a> guide covers what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner with a modest yard and an existing set of batteries, the practical answer is usually to stay on the platform you have. Both brands' outdoor tools are capable for typical suburban lots, and avoiding a second battery system saves more money than any small performance difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Price, Warranty and Who Should Choose Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither brand is a budget platform. Both price their professional lines at the upper end of the market and both sell entry-level kits that are much better value, often bundled with batteries. Prices move constantly with promotions, especially around holidays, so compare current kit prices for the exact tools you want rather than relying on a general rule about which brand is cheaper. Buying a kit with batteries and a charger is almost always cheaper than buying the same items separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            On warranty, Milwaukee's standard tool coverage is 5 years for most tools, which is longer than Makita's 3-year limited US warranty. Makita's 3-year terms cover tools, batteries and chargers on its lithium-ion platforms in the US, and most M18 REDLITHIUM packs also carry 3 years. Warranty terms vary by product and by country, so check the warranty card for any tool you buy, and register it if the brand asks you to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose Milwaukee if you work in a trade where its specialty tools matter, especially plumbing, electrical or automotive work, if you want the highest-torque tools in core categories, or if M12's compact trade tools suit the spaces you work in. The longer tool warranty is a bonus for heavy professional use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose Makita if you use tools for long days and value low weight, balance and low vibration, if you want the broadest possible range of tools and yard equipment on one 18V battery, or if you work in woodworking and finish carpentry where Makita's range is especially deep. Accept that XGT is a separate battery and only buy into it when you need its power.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own batteries from either brand, stay with them unless a specific tool forces a change. The cost of switching platforms almost always outweighs the difference between two good brands. The same logic applies to supporting tools such as a <a href="/best-cordless-shop-vac/">cordless shop vac</a>, where the best choice is simply the good one on the batteries you already have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Brand Instead of a Platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You are buying into a battery system, not a single tool. Before deciding, list the tools you will buy over the next few years and check that each exists on M18 or LXT at a price you accept. A brand that wins on drills but lacks a tool you need is the wrong choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Makita XGT Batteries Fit LXT Tools</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Makita's 40V XGT and 18V LXT batteries are separate systems. An adaptor lets XGT chargers charge LXT packs, but LXT batteries do not run XGT tools. Only buy XGT when you need its extra power, and plan for two battery types if you do.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching Platforms for a Small Advantage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you already own batteries from either brand, the cost of switching usually outweighs any difference between them. Stay with your platform unless a specific tool you need only exists on the other one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Warranty Registration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Warranty terms differ by brand, product and country, and some coverage depends on registration and proof of purchase. Register tools when you buy them and keep the receipt so a failure becomes a claim, not a replacement you pay for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Third-Party Battery Adapters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adapters that let one brand's batteries run another brand's tools bypass the electronic communication that protects both from overheating and over-discharge. They void warranties and create a safety risk, so avoid them even when they look like a cheap way to mix brands.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee better than Makita?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better overall. Milwaukee leads on trade-specific tools and top-end torque, and has a longer standard tool warranty at 5 years. Makita leads on weight, balance, low vibration and the breadth of its 18V LXT range, including outdoor equipment. Choose by the tools you need and how you work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Milwaukee and Makita batteries interchangeable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Milwaukee M18 and Makita LXT batteries use different designs and electronic interfaces. Third-party adapters exist, but they void warranties and bypass safety communication between pack and tool. Within Makita, LXT and XGT batteries are also separate systems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which has the better warranty, Milwaukee or Makita?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Milwaukee's standard tool warranty is 5 years for most tools, compared with Makita's 3-year limited warranty in the US. Batteries are closer: most M18 REDLITHIUM packs carry 3 years and Makita's lithium-ion batteries and chargers carry 3 years. Terms vary by product and country, so check each tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Milwaukee Tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Milwaukee Tool is owned by Techtronic Industries, the same group that owns Ryobi and RIDGID's power tools. Despite the shared owner, Milwaukee's M18 and M12 batteries are not compatible with Ryobi or RIDGID batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Makita XGT worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you need its extra power for heavy tools such as large saws, rotary hammers or outdoor equipment, or you are starting fresh in those categories. For most everyday drilling and fastening, 18V LXT is the better choice because its range is much larger and the batteries are cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a homeowner, Milwaukee or Makita?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Either is a good long-term choice. A homeowner who wants one battery for workshop tools and yard equipment may lean toward Makita's broad LXT range, while one who values maximum power in a drill and impact driver may lean toward Milwaukee. Compare current kit prices, which change often.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee or Makita better for woodworking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Makita is often the preferred choice for woodworking and finish carpentry because of its light, low-vibration tools and its wide range of specialty tools such as planers and trim routers. Milwaukee's M18 range covers woodworking well too, so check that the specific tools you want exist on your chosen platform.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

